3	//! minix.rs `memory` driver — the boot ramdisk, read-only (slice 5.7, decision D3).
4	//!
5	//! The first *block* device in minix.rs, and the storage MFS lands on in slice 5.8.
6	//! `kernel/build.rs` builds a MinixFS v3 image at compile time and packs it into
7	//! the MXBI archive as a non-ELF blob; the kernel copies it into RAM frames and
8	//! maps them into this driver's address space at [`RAMDISK_VA`] while building it.
9	//! This driver asks where (`SYS_GETINFO(GET_RAMDISK)`, which only it may call) and
10	//! then serves `BDEV_READ`.
11	//!
12	//! MINIX 3 boots the same shape, and Phase 6 swaps virtio-blk in underneath an
13	//! unchanged MFS — which is why nothing here knows what a filesystem is.
14	//!
15	//! ## Four things worth knowing
16	//!
17	//! **This driver never dereferences the mapping, and contains no `unsafe` block.**
18	//! (The only `unsafe` tokens in the crate are the two ELF-only attributes on
19	//! `_start`, which every freestanding binary here carries.) A client transfer is
20	//! `SYS_SAFECOPY`, and even the boot self-check goes through `SYS_COPY(SELF →
21	//! SELF)` rather than a raw load. So a page the kernel failed to map surfaces as
22	//! an `EFAULT` *return value* from a kernel call — a better diagnostic than TTY's
23	//! equivalent, which would be an EL0 data abort — and there is no MMIO sibling
24	//! module and no new Sonar exclusion.
25	//!
26	//! **The granter comes from `m_source`.** This driver holds `SYS_SAFECOPY`; its
27	//! clients do not. A granter field in the payload would let any client aim a
28	//! privileged cross-address-space copy at a third party's memory through it.
29	//! `bdev.rs` has no way to express one.
30	//!
31	//! **An over-long or out-of-range request is `EINVAL`.** Not a short read (a
32	//! filesystem cannot interpret half a block) and not `EIO` (which stays reserved
33	//! for Phase 6's real media errors). See `bdev.rs`.
34	//!
35	//! **`BDEV_WRITE` answers `EROFS`, not `ENOSYS`.** `ENOSYS` is already the
36	//! unknown-`m_type` answer, so reusing it would make "never heard of writes" and
37	//! "knows about writes and refuses them" indistinguishable. Slice 5.10 changes one
38	//! line inside that arm.
39	
40	// Freestanding for the real (bare-metal) build, but a normal host binary under
41	// `cargo test` so `bdev`'s logic gets host-runnable unit tests. The test harness
42	// needs `std` and its own entry point, so `no_std`/`no_main` and the `_start` shim
43	// below are all gated to `not(test)`.
44	#![cfg_attr(not(test), no_std)]
45	#![cfg_attr(not(test), no_main)]
46	
47	minixrs_abi_note::brand!();
48	
49	mod bdev;
50	
51	use minixrs_ipc::ipc_send;
52	use minixrs_kernel_shared::Message;
53	use minixrs_kernel_shared::callnr::{
54	    BDEV_BLOCK_SIZE, BDEV_READ, BDEV_WRITE, GET_RAMDISK, GETINFO_RAMDISK_LEN_OFF,
55	    GETINFO_RAMDISK_VA_OFF, SAFECOPY_TO, SYS_GETINFO_NAME_LEN,
56	};
57	use minixrs_kernel_shared::endpoint::{Endpoint, SELF};
58	use minixrs_kernel_shared::error::{ENOSYS, EROFS, OK};
59	use minixrs_kernel_shared::rootfs::{
60	    HDR_BLOCKS_OFF, IMAGE_HDR_LEN, IMAGE_LABEL, IMAGE_LABEL_LEN, IMAGE_TAIL_LABEL,
61	};
62	use minixrs_kernel_shared::uspace::RAMDISK_VA;
63	use minixrs_server_rt::{
64	    SefConfig, buf_addr, diag_fmt, rd_u64, sef_publish_to_ds, sef_startup, sys_copy, sys_getinfo,
65	    sys_safecopy,
66	};
